    function debounce(func, wait) {
      let timeout;

      return function() {
        clearTimeout(timeout);
        timeout = setTimeout(func, wait);
      };
    }

    // Example usage:
    const inputHandler = debounce(() => {
      console.log('Input handler called!');
    }, 300);

    document.querySelector('input').addEventListener('input', inputHandler);

In this implementation, we create a function called `debounce` that takes two arguments: `func`, which is the function to be debounced, and `wait`, which is the number of milliseconds to wait before calling the function again. The returned function is what will be used as the event listener.

When the returned function is called, it first clears any existing timeout (if there is one), then sets a new timeout using `setTimeout`. The timeout will call the original function after the specified wait time has elapsed.

The key difference between debouncing and throttling is that debouncing will only call the function once all input has stopped, while throttling will call the function at most once per wait time

